      "preview_head": "to follow the history along from there until as close to today as I can manage, hopefully getting in a few of the basics of the workings of these strange and wonderful machines. After Babbage\u2019s death ",
      "preview_tail": "by proposing a simple abstract device that could simulate any computer algorithm. Another pivotal figure is John von Neumann, whose architecture forms the basis for nearly every digital computer built",
      "full_text": "to follow the history along from there until as close to today as I can manage, hopefully getting in a few of the basics of the workings of these strange and wonderful machines. After Babbage\u2019s death as a relatively unknown and unloved mathematician in 1871, the progress of the science of computing continued to tick over. A Dublin accountant named Percy Ludgate, independently of Babbage\u2019s work, did develop his own programmable, mechanical computer at the turn of the century (around 1909), but sadly like Babbage's machines, it was never built due to a lack of funding and resources. This period saw limited progress in actually constructing these grand ideas into reality. The real breakthrough came in the early 20th century with the work of Alan Turing, who introduced his concept of the Universal Machine (now known as the Turing machine) in 1936. Turing's theoretical model laid the groundwork for modern computing by proposing a simple abstract device that could simulate any computer algorithm. Another pivotal figure is John von Neumann, whose architecture forms the basis for nearly every digital computer built",
      "avg_prediction": 0.4985,
